==English==
{{was wotd|2016|September|11}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|remembraunce}}

===Etymology===
Frm {{der|en|fro|remembrance}}, from {{m|fro|remembrer||to [[remember]]}}, from {{der|en|LL.|rememorārī||to [[call to mind]], to remember}}. Equivalent to {{suffix|remember|ance|lang=en}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|/ɹɪ.ˈmɛm.bɹəns/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|En-us-remembrance.ogg|Audio (GA)|lang=en}}
* {{hyphenation|re|mem|brance|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# The act of [[remember]]ing; a holding in mind, or bringing to mind; [[recollection]].
#* {{quote-book|author=John Milton|authorlink=John Milton|chapter={{w|Samson Agonistes}}|title=[[w:Paradise Regained|Paradise Regain’d]]. A Poem. In IV Books. To which is Added Samson Agonistes|location=London|publisher=Printed by J. M[acock]. for John Starkey at the Mitre in [[w:Fleet Street|Fleetstreet]], near [[w:Temple Bar, London|Temple-Bar]]|date=29 May 1671|page=59, lines 952–954|pageurl=https://archive.org/stream/paradiseregaindp00milt_0#page/59/mode/1up/|oclc=228732398|passage={{smallcaps|[[w:Delilah|Dal[ila]]]}}. Let me approach at leaſt, and touch thy hand. / {{smallcaps|[[w:Samson|Sams[on]]]}}. Not for thy life, leſt fierce '''remembrance''' wake / my ſudden rage to tear thee joint by joint.}}
#* '''1705''', {{w|Joseph Addison}}, “Bolonia, Modena, Parma, Turin, &c.”, in ''Remarks on Several Parts of Italy, &c. in the Years 1701, 1702, 1703'', London: Printed for {{w|Jacob Tonson}}, <small>{{w|OCLC}} [http://www.worldcat.org/oclc/181833922 181833922]</small>; republished The Hague: Printed for Henry Scheurleer, 1718, <small>{{w|OCLC}} [http://www.worldcat.org/oclc/224641578 224641578]</small>, [https://books.google.com/books?id=znQPAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA292 page 292]:
#*: For ''{{l|en|Titan|id=mythology}}'', by the mightly Loſs [of {{l|en|Phaëthon}}] diſmay'd, / Among the Heav'ns th'Immortal Fact diſplay'd, / Leſt the '''remembrance''' of his Grief ſhould fail, / And in Conſtellations wrote his Tale. [Translation of a work by {{w|Claudian}}.]
# The state of being remembered, or held in mind; [[memory]], recollection.
#* {{quote-book|author=William Shakespeare|authorlink=William Shakespeare|chapter=[[w:Hamlet|The Tragedie of Hamlet, Prince of Denmarke]]|title=[[w:First Folio|Mr. William Shakespeares Comedies, Histories, & Tragedies: Published According to the True Originall Copies]]|location=London|publisher=Printed by [[w:William Jaggard|Isaac Iaggard]], and [[w:Edward Blount|Ed[ward] Blount]]|year=1599–1602|year_published=1623|section=Act IV, scene v|page=274|pageurl=https://books.google.com.sg/books?id=uNtBAQAAMAAJ&pg=RA1-PA274|passage=There's Roſemary, that's for '''Remembraunce'''. / Pray loue remember: and there is [[pansy|Paconcies]], that's for Thoughts.}}
#* '''1678''', {{w|John Bunyan}}, ''[[w:The Pilgrim's Progress|The Pilgrim's Progress from This World, to That Which is to Come: Delivered under the Similitude of a Dream wherein is Discovered, the Manner of His Setting Out, His Dangerous Journey; and Safe Arrival at the Desired Countrey]]'', London: Printed for Nath[aniel] Ponder at the Peacock in the [[w:Poultry, London|Poultrey]] near [[w:Cornhill, London|Cornhill]], <small>{{w|OCLC}} [http://www.worldcat.org/oclc/733063856 733063856]</small>; republished as ''The Pilgrim's Progress as Originally Published by John Bunyan: Being a Fac-simile Reproduction of the First Edition'', London: Elliot Stock, 1875, <small>{{w|OCLC}} [http://www.worldcat.org/oclc/34741916 34741916]</small>, [https://books.google.com/books?id=-QD3V0dpUhsC&pg=PA77 page 77]:
#*: Yes, and did ſee ſuch things there, the '''remembrance''' of which will ſtick by me as long as I live; ſpecially three things, ''to wit'', How Chriſt, in deſpite of Satan, maintains his work of Grace in the heart; how the Man had ſinned himſelf quite out of hopes of Gods mercy; and alſo the Dream of him that thought in his ſleep the day of Judgement was come.
#* '''1725–1726''', {{w|Homer}}; {{w|Alexander Pope}} and {{w|William Broome}}, transl., ''[[w:Odyssey|The Odyssey of Homer. Translated from the Greek]]'', London: Printed for [[w:Barnaby Bernard Lintot|Bernard Lintot]], <small>{{w|OCLC}} [http://www.worldcat.org/oclc/8736646 8736646]</small>; republished as [[w:Henry Francis Cary|H[enry] F[rancis] Cary]], editor, ''The Odyssey of Homer: Translated by Alexander Pope'', London: [[w:Routledge|George Routledge and Sons]], the Broadway, {{w|Ludgate}}; New York, N.Y.: 416, {{w|Broome Street}}, 1872, <small>{{w|OCLC}} [http://www.worldcat.org/oclc/880970094 880970094]</small>, book VIII, [https://books.google.com/books?id=Nv4pAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA381 page 381]:
#*: {{...}} {{w|Nausicaa}} blooming as a goddess stands, / With wondering eyes the hero &#91;{{w|Odysseus}}&#93; she survey'd / And grateful thus began the royal maid: / 'Hail, godlike stranger! and when heaven restores / To thy fond wish thy long-expected shores, / This, ever grateful, in '''remembrance''' bear, / To me thou owest, to me, the vital air.'
#* {{quote-book|author=Jane Austen|authorlink=Jane Austen|title=[[w:Pride and Prejudice|Pride and Prejudice: A Novel. In Three Volumes]]|location=London|publisher=Printed [by George Sidney] for [[w:Thomas Egerton (publisher)|T[homas] Egerton]], Military Library, {{w|Whitehall}}|year=1813|volume=III|page=279|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=n0gJAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA279|oclc=38659585|passage=The letter, perhaps, began in bitterness, but it did not end so. The adieu is charity itself. But think no more of the letter. The feelings of the person who wrote, and the person who received it, are now so widely different from what they were then, that every unpleasant circumstance attending it ought to be forgotten. You must learn some of my philosophy. Think only of the past as its '''remembrance''' gives you pleasure.}}
# Something remembered; a person or thing kept in memory.
# That which serves to keep in or bring to mind; a [[memento]], a [[memorial]], a [[souvenir]], a [[token]]; a [[memorandum]] or [[note#Noun|note]] of something to be remembered.
#* {{quote-book|author=William Shakespeare|authorlink=William Shakespeare|chapter={{w|The Two Gentlemen of Verona}}|title=[[w:First Folio|Mr. William Shakespeares Comedies, Histories, & Tragedies: Published According to the True Originall Copies]]|location=London|publisher=Printed by [[w:William Jaggard|Isaac Iaggard]], and [[w:Edward Blount|Ed[ward] Blount]]|year=1589–1593|year_published=1623|section=Act II, scene ii|page=24|pageurl=https://books.google.com.sg/books?id=uNtBAQAAMAAJ&pg=PA24|passage=If you turne not: you will returne the ſooner: / Keep this '''remembrance''' for thy ''Iulia''’s sake.}}
#* '''1590''', {{w|Edmund Spenser}}, ''[[w:The Faerie Queene|The Faerie Qveene. Disposed into Twelue Books, Fashioning XII. Morall Vertues]]'', London: Printed for [[w:William Ponsonby (publisher)|W[illiam] Ponsonbie]], <small>{{w|OCLC}} [http://www.worldcat.org/oclc/18024649 18024649]</small>, book I, canto I, stanzas I and II; republished in [[w:John Hughes (poet)|John Hughes]], editor, ''The Works of Mr. Edmund Spenser. In Six Volumes. With a Glossary Explaining the Old and Obscure Words'', volume I, London: Printed for {{w|Jacob Tonson}} at Shakespear's Head, over against Catherine-street in the [[w:Strand, London|Strand]], 1715, <small>{{w|OCLC}} [http://www.worldcat.org/oclc/175074 175074]</small>, [https://books.google.com/books?id=SuM4AQAAMAAJ&pg=PA23 page 23]:
#*: [stanza I] A Gentle Knight was pricking on the Plain, / Yclad in mightie Arms and ſilver Shield, {{...}} / [stanza II] And on his Breaſt a bloody Croſs he bore, / The dear '''remembrance''' of his dying Lord, / For whoſe ſweet ſake that glorious Badge he wore, / And dead (as living) ever him ador'd: {{...}}
# The power of remembering; the reach of personal knowledge; the period over which one's memory extends.
#* '''1667''', {{w|John Milton}}, ''[[w:Paradise Lost|Paradise Lost. A Poem Written in Ten Books]]'', London: Printed [by {{w|Samuel Simmons}}], and are to be sold by Peter Parker under Creed Church neer {{w|Aldgate}}; and by Robert Boulter at the Turks Head in [[w:Bishopsgate|Bishopsgate-street]]; and Matthias Walker, under [[w:St Dustan-in-the-West|St. Dunstons Church]] in [[w:Fleet Street|Fleet-street]], <small>{{w|OCLC}} [http://www.worldcat.org/oclc/767532218 767532218]</small>, book VIII; republished as ''Paradise Lost. A Poem in Twelve Books'', London: Printed for C. Hitch and L. Hawes, [[w:Jacob Tonson|J[acob] and R[ichard] Tonson]], B. Dod, [[w:Rivington (publishers)|J[ohn] Rivington]], J. Ward, J. Richardson, S. Crowder and Co., [[w:Thomas Longman|T[homas] Longman]], E. Dilly, and A. and C. Corbet, 1760, <small>{{w|OCLC}} [http://www.worldcat.org/oclc/946737211 946737211]</small>, [https://books.google.com/books?id=fzZYAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA211 page 211, lines 203–205]:
#*: Thee I have heard relating what was done / Ere my '''remembrance'''; now hear me relate / My ſtory, which perhaps thou haſt not heard; {{...}}
